<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-two-real-eigenvalues" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Case of Two Distinct Real Eigenvalues</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       RECAP: TWO PAIRS, TWO SOLUTIONS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Suppose our matrix <m>A</m> falls into the first case, where it has two distinct, real
    eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1</m> and <m>\lambda_2</m>, with corresponding eigenvectors
    <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m>. Then <xref ref="thm-eigenvector-solution"/>
    gives us two solutions to <m>\frac{d\mathbf{x}}{dt} = A\mathbf{x}</m>:
    <me>\mathbf{z}(t) = \mathbf{v}_1 e^{\lambda_1 t}
      \quad \text{and} \quad
      \mathbf{y}(t) = \mathbf{v}_2 e^{\lambda_2 t}.</me>
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       LINEAR ALGEBRA FACT + THE CHAIN TO SUPERPOSITION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    To make use of these two solutions, we need one more fact, which we borrow from linear
    algebra (we won't justify it here).
  </p>

  <fact xml:id="fact-distinct-eigenvectors-independent">
    <title>Eigenvectors From Distinct Eigenvalues are Linearly Independent</title>
    <p>
      Suppose <m>A</m> has two real distinct eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1</m> and
      <m>\lambda_2</m>, and that <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> are
      corresponding eigenvectors. Then <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> are
      linearly independent vectors.
    </p>
  </fact>

  <p>
    Since <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> are
    linearly independent, one can show that the Wronskian of our two solutions is never
    zero:
    <me>W(\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{y}) \neq 0.</me>
    And by <xref ref="thm-wronskian-general-solution"/>, two solutions with non-zero
    Wronskian combine, through superposition, into the general solution:
    <me>c_1 \mathbf{z} + c_2 \mathbf{y}.</me>
    Putting all the pieces together gives us the main theorem of this case.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-two-real-eigenvalues">
    <title>The Eigenvalue Method for Two Distinct Real Eigenvalues</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>A</m> is a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix with two distinct, real eigenvalues
        <m>\lambda_1, \lambda_2</m> with corresponding eigenvectors
        <m>\mathbf{v}_1, \mathbf{v}_2</m>. Then the general solution of
        <me>\frac{d\mathbf{x}}{dt} = A\mathbf{x}</me>
        is
        <me>\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\mathbf{v}_1 e^{\lambda_1 t} + c_2\mathbf{v}_2 e^{\lambda_2 t}.</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       WORKED EXAMPLE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Now let's do a quick example applying this theorem. Luckily, we have previously found the eigenvalues and eigenvectors
     to the matrix we will use!
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-solve-two-real">
    <title>Solving a System with Two Distinct Real Eigenvalues</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the general solution of the system
        <md>
          <mrow>x_1' \amp= 4x_1 + 2x_2</mrow>
          <mrow>x_2' \amp= 3x_1 - x_2.</mrow>
        </md>
        You may use the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the coefficient matrix
        <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, which we found in
        the last section:
        <me>\lambda_1 = -2 \text{ with } \mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          \lambda_2 = 5 \text{ with } \mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Remember the two steps:
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Plug each eigenvalue-eigenvector pair into <m>\mathbf{v}e^{\lambda t}</m> to
              get two solutions.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Apply superposition to get the general solution.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        First, each eigenvalue-eigenvector pair gives a solution:
        <md>
          <mrow>\lambda_1 = -2 \text{ with } \mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}
            \amp\implies
            \mathbf{z}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}e^{-2t}</mrow>
          <mrow>\lambda_2 = 5 \text{ with } \mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \amp\implies
            \mathbf{y}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}e^{5t}.</mrow>
        </md>
        Second, since the eigenvalues are distinct and real, superposition gives the
        general solution:
        <me>\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}e^{-2t}
          + c_2\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}e^{5t}.</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: ASSEMBLE A GENERAL SOLUTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Now you try assembling a general solution. It should be quick once the eigenvalues and eigenvectors are established!
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="act-assemble-general-solution">
    <title>Assembling the General Solution</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose a matrix <m>B</m> has eigenvalues and eigenvectors
        <me>\lambda_1 = 3 \text{ with } \mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          \lambda_2 = -1 \text{ with } \mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}.</me>
        What is the general solution of
        <m>\dfrac{d\mathbf{x}}{dt} = B\mathbf{x}</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}e^{3t}
              + c_2\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}e^{-t}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! Each eigenvector is paired with its own eigenvalue, and the two
            solutions are combined with the arbitrary constants <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>,
            exactly as in <xref ref="thm-two-real-eigenvalues"/>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}e^{-t}
              + c_2\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}e^{3t}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Careful: the eigenvalues and eigenvectors got mismatched. The eigenvector
            <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m> belongs to <m>\lambda_1 = 3</m>,
            so it should be paired with <m>e^{3t}</m>, not <m>e^{-t}</m>. Remember:
            eigenvalues and eigenvectors must go together!
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\mathbf{x}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}e^{3t}
              + \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}e^{-t}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Close, but this is just <em>one particular</em> solution. The general solution
            must describe <em>all</em> solutions, which requires the arbitrary constants
            <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> in front of each piece.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </activity>

  <p>
    As we have seen before, the general solution can also be combined into a single vector by
    expanding out the components. For example, the answer above can be written as
    <m>\mathbf{x}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} c_1 e^{3t} + c_2 e^{-t} \\ 2c_1 e^{3t} - c_2 e^{-t} \end{bmatrix}</m>.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Two pairs give two solutions:</alert> with two distinct real eigenvalues,
          the pairs <m>(\lambda_1, \mathbf{v}_1)</m> and <m>(\lambda_2, \mathbf{v}_2)</m>
          give the two solutions <m>\mathbf{v}_1 e^{\lambda_1 t}</m> and
          <m>\mathbf{v}_2 e^{\lambda_2 t}</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The general solution:</alert> since eigenvectors from distinct eigenvalues
          are linearly independent, the Wronskian is non-zero and superposition gives
          <m>\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\mathbf{v}_1 e^{\lambda_1 t} + c_2\mathbf{v}_2 e^{\lambda_2 t}</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Keep the pairs together:</alert> each eigenvector must be matched with its
          own eigenvalue in the exponent.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
